  message: "LEFT OPEN. The work is written and pushed as PR #92 (branch jgstern-agent/fix/wi-zafid-completeness-signal), but CI failed TWICE and I could not diagnose it, so I am not closing this.\n\nWHAT THE PR CONTAINS (ready to resume, do not redo):\n  (a) The asymmetry fix. finalize._finalize_skipped_into_limits keyed only on a run's files_skipped, so a parse-error drop set limits.partial_results_reason while an oversize drop — which lands only in limits.truncated_files, because add_truncated_file never touches files_skipped — set nothing. It now triggers on EITHER channel, with a non-vacuity control asserting the reason stays empty when nothing was dropped.\n  (b) The spec fix. See the ADJUDICATION below.\n  (c) Three tests in test_finalize.py: the truncated channel, the previously-covered files_skipped channel, and the no-drops control.\n\nADJUDICATION (the substantive finding, independent of whether the PR ever lands). The item's second half — 'analysis_incomplete never trips' — is CORRECT BEHAVIOUR and must not be 'fixed'. My first implementation bound the flag to the reason so the quoted rule ('partial_results_reason present only when analysis_incomplete: true') would hold. It passed its tests. It was WRONG: the spec's green-marked Analyzer-crashes section prescribes setting partial_results_reason AND states analysis_incomplete is not set, and the flag's own definition is 'terminated EARLY' while every fail-open drop explicitly continues. So the quoted constraint forbade a state the spec elsewhere mandates — NO implementation could satisfy both. That is a spec self-contradiction, not a code defect. I reverted and removed the constraint from the spec instead. GENERALIZABLE: when an item quotes a spec rule as violated, read every OTHER passage governing the same field before picking a side; the quoted one may be the defect.\n\nTHE CI BLOCKER, stated honestly. Two runs, both 'ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker: failure', and the log is NOT retrievable — 'CI logs are hosted in Woodpecker (behind Cloudflare Access) and are not retrievable via the API'. That is WI-solob exactly, and WI-solob is a GOVERNANCE file needing owner approval, so I could not clear it.\n\nELIMINATED LOCALLY (so the next session does not repeat this):\n  - tests: 1598 passed / 1 skipped across the same committed 36-file manifest\n  - changed-file coverage: finalize.py 100% (0 missed) under that manifest — the gate CI enforces\n  - all five verify-generated --check gates green; 17 pre-commit checks green\n  - the three spec-referencing tests the import slice does NOT select (test_ir, test_handler_slices, test_repo_fingerprint): 220 passed\n  - root-sensitivity (pt.37 chmod trap): the only PermissionError case in the manifest uses monkeypatch, not chmod, so it is euid-independent\n  - mypy ratchet: non-blocking, and its +4 is WI-basat's known baseline drift (1083 recorded vs 1087 measured), not this change\n  - a manifest-drift-after-rebase hypothesis was attempted but INCONCLUSIVE: the regeneration timed out at 500s, so that comparison proved nothing. It remains the most plausible untested cause and is where I would start.\n\nNEXT STEP: get the Woodpecker log (needs WI-solob, or a human opening https://ci.revolving-tactile.com.de/repos/1/pipeline/287/1). Do not re-guess blind — two blind attempts already cost a full CI cycle each."  # c13f
